alyze in Fiddlesticks it's pretty  service level stuff which allowed people  of all ages and backgrounds to quickly  find the appeal in it again the same  thing goes for cup hit the surface  appeal is immense as its recreating and  recapturing the magic of the dawn of  cartoons for a lot of people and it's  giving players  the opportunity to authentically join in  that cartoon fund for the first time  now this isn't as crucial as the visuals  in my opinion but the music absolutely  needs to be brought up the soundtrack  for the game is a collection of jazz  ballads with a mix of big band and  smaller groups it's done fantastically  first all the music by itself is catchy  and well done I wouldn't be surprised to  find any of these songs populating the  radio waves of 1930 to 1960 America you  guys are familiar with the Fallout  series I wouldn't be surprised to hear a  lot of these songs on the fallout radio  and even more all of the music is  extremely catered to the game there are  the songs which directly reference  things in the game like don't deal with  the devil which gives the game's  exposition the barbershop quartet or Die  house which serves as King dies killer  theme song but you can tell that a lot  of the boss soundtracks are clearly  designed for the boss stage to example  the boss cagney incarnation moves in an  almost salsa like motion shaking its  arms back and forth while the song  floral fury plays the song is filled  with maracas rapid-fire trumpet in the  collage of Sachs's and drums all  combining into a reoccurring melody  which would be perfect for an intense  fast-paced dance which you could say is  going on between cut head and the boss  the dynamic of the music is shown in the  dynamic of the visuals what you see on  the screen what you hear come together  so that gives an overview of most of the  non gameplay features now I want to  discuss the controls level design and  combat gameplay and then we'll see how  these intersect or don't so