g arenas the  attacks were usually unique and fitting  to the boss I did say the control setup  is simple but that doesn't mean it's  deployment in the game is as well the  game can get very complex very fast if  you know what you're doing even if you  don't know what you're doing for that  matter playing too risky by moving into  zones in which it's easier to get hit  often reward the player by allowing them  for more damage with different weapons  like the spread shop it also rewards  ability to focus on offense while  playing defense the auto lean tracer  allows you to solely focus on dodging  the bullets will find their target for  you but this comes at a hefty cost in  low dam the dps for the chaser is only  fifteen point six this damage is dwarfed  by the strongest weapon in the game the  charge shop which has DPS of 40 but  requires aiming and delay for every  single shot meaning is a lot easier to  miss and if you don't charge it all the  way you're gonna get very poor damage  this means that a lot of focus is taken  away from Dodge  the game does not have much  customization but the dynamics in weapon  variety  fortunately allow for at least a few  different strategies while fighting  bosses on the solely defense side of  things there is essentially always a way  to avoid getting hit whether that be  through better positioning or being  ready to make a risky - the majority of  deaths will be your own fault which they  have to be or the game could not have  succeeded it's hard enough to get people  to play a game in which you fairly die a  lot it would be impossible to get people  to play a game in which you unfairly  dialogue you'll generally be able to see  what you did wrong and know what to do  better next time though speaking from  experience it could take 20 tries to  actually consistently make that better  move now this complexity and difficulty  is in my eyes very juxtaposed with  everything else the accessibility and  even familiarity of the silly cartoon  visuals and the groove and soundtrack is  at odds with a difficulty and the high  learning curve that the game reveals  this dichotomy acts to engage multiple  faculties of enjoyment while plain the  upbeat visuals and music brings the  player up while the bosses continually  beat the player down I'd say that the  most popular example of a similar  crushingly difficult game in recent  history is the souls franchise which  rather than employ dichotomy between  welcoming graphics and punishing  gameplay like cuphead does fully commits  to the difficulty of gameplay with a  complimentary dark and brooding world  I'm not saying one of these  methodologies is better or worse in  gaming but realizing the difference is  important if we want to understand  cuphead and appreciate it for what it is  I'd even go to say most games take the  dark souls approach of making gameplay  and everything else complimentary it's a  lot more straightforward of an approach  and that really really works Dark Souls  is an amazing series and the combination  of similar themes between gameplay and  audio visuals really hits the overall  feel of the game home the dark world and  ominous music adds to the tough combat  to create a very rough atmosphere and an  almost sinister tone but having a game  that instead puts its gameplay at odds  with other features allows us a  different sort of feeling and enjoyment  cuphead in particular activates not only  our appreciation of difficulty but also  our more silly enjoyment and  Staal juh of things like Saturday  morning cartoons if you know anything  about my channel you know I fully  appreciate the souls approach it lets  developers evoke deeper levels of  specific feelings much more so than a  game like puppet but come pet simply  offers something that is not found as  frequently  fortunately the developers did a very  good job of pulling off this dichotomy  so it avoids a potential pitfall a  feeling shallow in both gameplay and  everything else instead it pairs solid  typical gameplay with more upbeat  visuals music and level design create a  game that lets us experience a wide  range of emotions and enjoyment at once  so that's really my main point in the  video is seeing that other side of  things that we don't normally see in  games but I still have two things I want  to bring up and I didn't really have a  great spot to put them previously those  things are the expressions of characters  throughout this game and Harry the  expressions are really the only time I  think the visuals line up with the  combat and pairing is to me a microcosm  of the dichotomy that I've gone over  some expressions almost every set of  eyes in the game even cup heads and mug  man's are aggressive and combative the  bosses take very menacing looks like  they really want to send you to the  afterlife the way they're drawn makes it  look like they're having fun with the  violence as well this still connects  with the old solid cartoons as animators  back in the day and now definitely would  have wanted their characters to look  like they were having fun to make sure  audience had fun with them button  cuphead this reveling in the fight is  the secluded example of some other  feature lining up to a tee with the  gameplay in combat I think it's a very  well-done exception so lastly I think  pre-fitting is pairing the point of this  video was to look at how gameplay is  juxtaposed with the rest of the game and  cuphead to bring about different  emotions in the player at the same time  pairing represents this and most of how  I've seen other content creators talk  about pairing I think supports this  claim it's always seen as a pretty weird  thing so almost any parable object is  something that can damage you you have  to jump and jump again into the object  that you're rewarded with another  playing card for special moves  you'll and the berries if you fail you  take damage so jumping into damaging  items is a bit unintuitive in the first  place but then to booth every parable  object is pink pink is a kind of a leaf  interesting color choice for this pink  is symbolically a delicate color that  represents things like sweet nice  playful this historically at odds with  damaging projectiles that can lead to  death or put you in a bad position the  positive and negative feelings wrapped  up in pairing are a more clear example  of the same phenomena happening through  the entire game and it's pretty cool to  me  that's why I made the video so that  about wraps up all of my thoughts on  cuphead in terms of gameplay versus  everything else this was somewhat like a  review but not quite since I was more  focusing on I guess I'd say part of the  game it wasn't a comprehensive review I  hope you enjoy it I do want to do more  analysis like this so if you like this  kind of stuff let me know however you  want to and thanks again for watching  have a good day
 

